;; For consumers that use the Clojure CLI: `{:local/root "../replique-cljs"}` puts
;; the source on their classpath directly, with no `mvn install` step in between,
;; so an edit here is picked up by their next REPL.
;;
;; The Maven build (pom.xml) is the one that runs the tests and builds a jar; this
;; file exists only so tools.deps can see the source tree.
;;
;; CLOJURE IS NOT NAMED HERE, deliberately. The compiler runs on replique-clj, and
;; it is the CONSUMER that must name it, because it stands in for
;; org.clojure/clojure - which the Clojure CLI's root deps.edn always puts on the
;; classpath, and which only the consuming project can override.
{;; THE ONE LIBRARY DEPENDENCY, and it is a ClojureScript one rather than a JVM
 ;; one: nothing in this compiler calls it. cljs/reader.cljs is vendored beside
 ;; cljs/core.cljs and opens (:require [cljs.tools.reader :as treader]
 ;; [cljs.tools.reader.edn :as edn]), so the seven cljs/tools/reader*.cljs files in
 ;; this jar are sources the COMPILER has to be able to find for a program that
 ;; reads edn - and clojure/edn.cljs, which is three delegating functions over
 ;; cljs.reader, is the name most programs reach for.
 ;;
 ;; Named here as well as in pom.xml, and that is the point: a consumer arriving by
 ;; :local/root gets :paths and :deps out of this file and never reads the pom, so
 ;; a pom-only declaration would leave cljs.reader shipped and unloadable on
 ;; exactly the path replique uses.
 ;;
 ;; 1.3.6 is the version ClojureScript 1.12.145 pins, for the same reason the
 ;; google-closure-library version is that one: the vendored cljs/reader.cljs was
 ;; written against it.
 :deps {org.clojure/tools.reader {:mvn/version "1.3.6"}}

 ;; src/clj FIRST, and target/classes only for what is not in it: the websocket
 ;; framing under clojure.cljs.ws is Java (src/java), so the only build of it is
 ;; Maven's and a consumer reading this source tree has nowhere else to find it -
 ;; without it clojure.cljs.browser will not load and a browser REPL is missing.
 ;; Maven also copies the .clj sources in there, which is why the order matters:
 ;; the live ones shadow the copies rather than the other way round.
 ;; src/compat LAST, and it is not the compiler. It holds TWO namespaces named the
 ;; way ClojureScript names them, for two different reasons.
 ;;
 ;; cljs.analyzer.api, because a macro namespace of somebody else's requires it BY
 ;; THAT NAME off the classpath, and this compiler renamed it to
 ;; clojure.cljs.analyzer-api. Without the file such a library does not half-work,
 ;; it does not load: `Could not locate cljs/analyzer/api.cljc on classpath', which
 ;; says nothing about a fork. hx's useSmartEffect is the one that turns up.
 ;;
 ;; cljs.tagged-literals, because what somebody else's macro wants from it is a
 ;; CLASS - cljs.tagged_literals.JSValue, the marker #js reads to, which
 ;; core.async's ioc_macros.clj imports and asks instance? about while it rewrites
 ;; a (go ...) body. That one is ClojureScript's own file, vendored verbatim and
 ;; forwarding nothing, and it is HERE rather than in src/clj for a reason of the
 ;; same shape as the one below and not the same reason: a copy in src/clj loads
 ;; from source into a DynamicClassLoader while the jar's AOT cljs.analyzer
 ;; resolves that name to the class in the jar, which is two classes with one name
 ;; in one jvm. See doc/cljs-compiler.md 5.68.
 ;;
 ;; THE DOCUMENTED DOOR AND ONLY IT, which is a fact about shape rather than a
 ;; preference: cljs.analyzer.api declares no dynamic var - it reads its own
 ;; through current-file, current-ns and current-state - so all of it forwards,
 ;; while cljs.analyzer is mostly dynamic vars, and a var cannot be forwarded
 ;; at all (Namespace.findInternedVar refuses a var whose ns is not this one).
 ;; Code
 ;; reaching through the internal name is asked to come through this one instead.
 ;;
 ;; A SOURCE ROOT OF ITS OWN BECAUSE IT MUST NOT BE ON OUR OWN TEST CLASSPATH,
 ;; and that is not tidiness - it is the whole reason the directory exists. The
 ;; tests carry ClojureScript 1.12.145 as an AST oracle
 ;; (clojure.cljs.test-harness/cljs-analyzer?), and the oracle IS cljs.analyzer:
 ;; clojure.cljs.oracle resolves cljs.analyzer/analyze, /empty-env, /*cljs-ns*,
 ;; /*analyze-deps*, /*load-macros*, and clojure.cljs.analyzer-test reaches for
 ;; cljs.analyzer/macroexpand-1 and cljs.analyzer.api/resolve. A .clj shadows a
 ;; jar's .cljc (RT.load tries .clj first), so putting this in src/clj turns that
 ;; last one into the oracle comparing this compiler with itself - a test suite
 ;; that passes and checks nothing. The file is not called analyzer.clj any more,
 ;; which makes the trap smaller and not gone: api.clj alone still does it.
 ;;
 ;; Nothing shadows for a CONSUMER, because a project using this compiler has no
 ;; ClojureScript on its classpath to shadow: its cljs/core.cljs would be a second
 ;; one beside ours, so it has to be excluded whether or not these files exist.
 :paths ["src/clj" "target/classes" "src/compat"]}
